The Rust provider creates applications, libraries, or a workspace containing one application/library package. Editions 2024 (default) and 2021 are available. Follow [Your first Rust project](/docs/first-rust-project/) for the creation walkthrough.

## Generated project

Every starter includes a dependency-free `src/lib.rs` with `add` and a unit test. Applications add `src/main.rs`, which prints `42`. A workspace places both under `crates/<name>/` and declares that package in the root `Cargo.toml`.

## Tasks

| Rivet task | Cargo operation |
| --- | --- |
| Configure | `metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps` |
| Check | `check --workspace` |
| Build | `build --workspace` |
| Test | `test --workspace` |
| Run | `run --package <package> --bin <binary> -- <arguments>` |
| Format | `fmt --all` |
| Lint | `clippy --workspace -- -D warnings` |
| Package | `package --workspace --no-verify` |

Release adds `--release` to Check, Build, Test, Run, and Lint. The local `jobs` option controls their build concurrency. Format requires rustfmt; Lint requires Clippy. Package creates Cargo packages without publishing or verifying their contents by compiling them.

Binary discovery uses `cargo metadata`. The picker identifies both package and binary; CLI selectors accept `package::binary` or an unambiguous binary name. Compiler JSON messages supply source locations for quickfix. These are Cargo's documented [editor integration interfaces](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/external-tools.html).

## Source and manifest ownership

`:RivetFile` creates a `.rs` file. A bare name goes under `src/`, inside the generated package for workspaces. Declare the module with `mod feature;` or `pub mod feature;` in its parent module to include it in compilation.

`:RivetTarget` adds an application under `src/bin/`. Additional libraries and workspace members belong in Cargo manifests. Use `cargo add`, `cargo remove`, or direct manifest edits for dependency changes.

Existing `Cargo.toml` projects work without `.rivet.json`; native tasks discover their binaries through Cargo. Cargo may update its lockfile or build output as part of those tasks. Runtime arguments and profiler commands are described in [Task options](/docs/task-options/).
